<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bengal Subah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1576">1576</a>–<a href="/years/1757">1757</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Mughal Bengal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subah">Subah</a> was the province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> covering Bengal, roughly present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a> and the Indian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>, at times with parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>. Conquered from the Karrani sultans in 1576, it became one of the empire's richest provinces, known for textiles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>. Its governors fought repeated wars with the Ahom kingdom, and from 1717 the Nawabs of Bengal ruled it with growing independence until the British victory at Plassey in 1757.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bengal-subah.jpg" alt="Bengal Subah" width="320" height="257" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Greatswrd · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bengal_Subah_Standard.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Mughal absorption of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> began under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>, who defeated Sultan Nasiruddin Nasrat Shah at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ghaghra">Battle of Ghaghra</a> in 1529, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a> briefly held the Bengali capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Gaur</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Sher Shah Suri</a> drove him out. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>'s army defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daud-khan-karrani">Daud Khan Karrani</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tukaroi">Battle of Tukaroi</a> on 3 March 1575, and after the Battle of Rajmahal Akbar made Bengal one of the original twelve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subah">subahs</a>. Local chiefs, among them the Baro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhuyan">Bhuyans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isa-khan">Isa Khan</a>, resisted for years; the historian Tapan Raychaudhuri dates the real pacification of the province from 1594.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The capital moved several times, from Tanda to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajmahal">Rajmahal</a>, renamed Akbarnagar, and in 1610 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaka">Dhaka</a>, renamed Jahangirnagar, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> built up with forts, gardens, palaces and mosques. Man Singh founded a coastal navy to contain Arakanese raids, Cooch Behar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamrup">Kamrup</a> were annexed in 1609 and 1612, and the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittagong">Chittagong</a> in 1666 took the port from the Kingdom of Arakan. Governors were often princes: Shah Shuja proclaimed himself emperor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> during the war of succession, lost to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> and was killed in Arakan, while Shaista Khan consolidated Mughal control of eastern Bengal.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murshid-quli-khan">Murshid Quli Khan</a>, the finance minister, gained control of the revenues and moved his office to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murshidabad">Murshidabad</a>; in 1717 his post became the hereditary title of nawab, and in 1740 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alivardi-khan">Alivardi Khan</a> seized power after the Battle of Giria. Maratha raids between 1741 and 1751 killed 400,000 Bengali civilians, among them weavers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> winders, and ended with Alivardi agreeing to pay the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauth">chauth</a>, an annual tribute of Rs. 1.2 million, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a> passed to the Marathas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>A Mughal subahdar governed with his own cabinet and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">diwans</a>, and deputies known as naib <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazim">nazims</a> ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> proper, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a>. Todar Mal's settlement of 1582 divided the province into 24 sarkars, and a landed aristocracy of jagirdars collected the revenue. Under the early nawabs Bengal supplied more than half the funds reaching the imperial treasury in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. The state cleared forest in the Bhati region for farmland, settled farmers in the delta and adapted the Bengali calendar for harvests and taxes, and peasants took up mulberry cultivation and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> was the official religion, while most of the population was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>; an estimated 30 million people lived there before the famine of 1770, which reduced them by as much as a third.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bengal Subah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
